================================================================================
POST-RUN ANALYSIS (2026-08-15) -- verdict against the pre-registered criteria
================================================================================

BATTERY VERDICT: FAIL. No beta is claimable from this run.
  B2 monotonicity  : FAIL for [sum] f=0.125
  B3' across f     : FAIL -- beta = +0.973 (f=1/8) vs +1.333 (f=1/4), |dbeta| = 0.36
  fit quality      : r^2 = 0.66 / 0.72 [sum]; windowed slopes disagree by ~50x
                     ([sum] f=0.125: +2.210 large-D half vs +0.042 small-D half)

DIAGNOSIS (arithmetic, not speculation): the baseline is NONZERO --
tau(D=0) = 0.993 (f=1/8) and 1.499 (f=1/4). Since tau(D) -> tau(0) != 0 as
D -> 0, log(tau) vs log(D) MUST flatten to slope 0 at small D whatever the
physics. A pure power law tau ~ D^beta is simply the wrong model: it forces
tau(0) = 0. The observed window signature (+2.21 vs +0.042) is exactly this.
The natural form is tau(D) = tau_0 + c*D^alpha, and the EXCESS DELAY
tau(D) - tau_0 is the quantity that could carry a clean exponent. Fitting
that here would be post-hoc; it requires its own pre-registration.

WHAT DOES SURVIVE -- the censoring result (owner ruling 2, reported first-class):
Censoring is PERFECTLY ORDERED. It always strikes the largest D first, and
strikes more often at the harder level:

    level        censored D values           attained
    [sum] f=1/8  0.2                         0.15 and below
    [sum] f=1/4  0.2, 0.15                   0.1 and below
    [max] f=1/8  0.2, 0.15                   0.1 and below
    [max] f=1/4  0.2, 0.15, 0.1              0.07 and below

No inversion anywhere: there is no D at which a larger-D configuration
thermalized while a smaller-D one did not. This is a clean ORDINAL result --
more dispersion, later thermalization, to the point where the strongest
dispersion never thermalizes within the horizon at all.

SIGN ROBUSTNESS: beta > 0 in all four fits, both conventions, both levels,
despite the poor fit quality -- and it agrees in direction with Option C's
independent evidence (dispersion suppresses peak enstrophy at both viscous
floors). Two methodologically different routes, same direction.

SUMMARY: the effect is REAL and DIRECTIONALLY ROBUST; it does NOT admit a
clean power-law exponent in this formulation. That is the honest state.
